---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { urls } from '../lib/enso';
import { ArrowUpRight, Database, ShieldCheck } from '@lucide/astro';
const sources = [
  { name: 'CPC ENSO Diagnostics Discussion', type: 'Official phase, alert, synopsis, and release dates', href: urls.discussion },
  { name: 'CPC weekly Niño-region SST', type: 'Weekly Niño 3.4 anomaly · 1991–2020 reference climatology', href: urls.weekly },
  { name: 'CPC Relative Oceanic Niño Index', type: 'Three-month RONI · ERSSTv6 and 1991–2020 reference', href: urls.roni },
  { name: 'CPC ENSO probabilities', type: 'Monthly issue · three-month RONI-based target seasons', href: urls.forecast },
  { name: 'CPC sea surface temperature figure', type: 'Original NOAA image · dates and scale retained', href: urls.map },
];
---
<Layout title="Data Sources & Methodology" active="methodology" description="How ENSOLens validates NOAA CPC ENSO observations and forecasts, preserves publication dates, and handles delayed or unavailable sources.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage">
    <div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">TRANSPARENT BY DESIGN</span><h1>Know what <span>you're looking at.</span></h1><p>The sources, the distinctions, and the limits behind the numbers.</p></div></div>
    <section class="method-section" id="indices"><span class="eyebrow">01 / THE INDICATORS</span><h2>Different numbers answer different questions.</h2><div class="index-grid"><article><span class="index-label">WEEKLY OBSERVATION</span><h3>Niño 3.4 anomaly</h3><p>A sea surface temperature anomaly for 5°N–5°S, 170°W–120°W. The weekly CPC series uses the 1991–2020 reference climatology. A weekly reading does not establish an official ENSO phase.</p></article><article><span class="index-label">SEASONAL RELATIVE INDEX</span><h3>RONI</h3><p>The Relative Oceanic Niño Index adjusts for tropical ocean background warming and uses a three-month window. CPC currently publishes ERSSTv6 RONI values against a 1991–2020 reference. Points appear at the end of each season; they are not weekly observations.</p></article><article><span class="index-label">HISTORICAL INDEX</span><h3>ONI</h3><p>The Oceanic Niño Index is a conventional three-month Niño 3.4 anomaly. It is not the weekly anomaly or RONI. ENSOLens charts RONI, and its probability table follows CPC's RONI-based framework.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="method-section"><span class="eyebrow">02 / ORIGINAL PUBLICATIONS</span><h2>Go back to the source.</h2><div class="source-list">{sources.map(source => <a href={source.href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><Database size={19} /><div><strong>{source.name}</strong><span>{source.type}</span></div><ArrowUpRight size={17} /></a>)}</div></section>
    <section class="method-section"><span class="eyebrow">03 / UPDATES & REVISIONS</span><h2>Keep the dates and uncertainty visible.</h2><div class="explanation-grid"><div><h3>Scheduled checks, dated observations.</h3><p>Sources are checked every six hours. This is a retrieval schedule, not the publication frequency of NOAA data. Each feed retains its own issue or observation date and retrieval time. Weekly anomalies and seasonal RONI are plotted on their own dates.</p><p>Validation checks source formats, dates, value ranges, and probability totals. If a source fails, its last validated data remains available and a notice appears. Checks delayed more than two days, overdue publications, and source failures are reported on the <a href="/api/status">data status endpoint</a>.</p></div><div><h3>Compare identical target seasons.</h3><p>Forecast comparison matches the same three-month season and year across issues. Missing target seasons remain blank. Monthly forecast revisions and map versions are retained, and CSV downloads identify the source series or forecast issue.</p><p>Probability is not impact severity. An ENSO phase probability is not a probability of flooding, snow, crop damage, or hurricane landfall. Regional pages describe historical tendencies, not current local forecasts.</p></div></div></section>
    <div class="source-callout"><ShieldCheck size={22} /><div><strong>Independent, with original sources one click away.</strong><p>ENSOLens republishes official data with independent presentation and context. It is not affiliated with NOAA, CPC, or the U.S. government. See <a href="/about/">publisher information</a> and <a href="/privacy/">privacy</a>.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</Layout>
